    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Rastislav asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> for teachers in 863 to free his church from Frankish priests; the mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> their first alphabet and liturgy.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> reached its greatest extent under Svatopluk I (870–894), when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> at least formally accepted his suzerainty.</li><li>Pope John VIII's bull Industriae tuae of 880 recognised Old Church Slavonic as the fourth liturgical language, beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>'s capital has never been found: the sources name a Veligrad, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikulcice">Mikulčice</a>, with its palace and twelve churches on an island in the Morava, is the most widely accepted candidate.</p></aside>
    </section>

