    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mojmir-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mojmir-i.jpg" alt="Mojmír I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mojmir-i">Mojmír I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mojmir_kniieza_Velkomoravske.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Slowakische Nationalbank, National Bank of Slovakia (Preßburg)(Slovenská národná banka, Bratislava); Jozef Vlček (painters); Peter Zelizňák, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> was the first major state that was predominantly West Slavic, centred on the Morava River, which gave it its name, in today's eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. The local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, long tributary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, built their first fortified settlements in the late seventh century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s campaigns in the 790s broke the Avar Khaganate, and Frankish swords and Carolingian art spread north of the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Fortresses at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, Rajhrad and Staré Město around 800 mark local centres of power.</p><p>The Royal Frankish Annals record “leaders of the Slavs who live along the Danube” at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>'s court in 811 and embassies of Moravians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 822, the earliest certain reference to them. The first named ruler is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mojmir-i">Mojmír I</a>, “duke of the Moravians”, who according to the Conversio Bagoariorum et Carantanorum expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribina">Pribina</a> across the Danube; Pribina fled to Ratpot, who administered the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> from around 833. Whether Pribina was an independent prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nitra</a> or Mojmír's lieutenant is disputed, but many historians hold that Great Moravia emerged through the enforced union of Nitra with Moravia in the early 830s. A thirteenth-century source says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Reginhar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> baptised “all of the Moravians” in 831. In 846 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> marched against Moravians “planning to defect” and installed Mojmír's nephew Rastislav as duke.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The nuclei of settlement were large fortified strongholds on hills, marshes and river islands, with ramparts of drystone facing and earth-filled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> frames, the elite in the central enclosure and crafts in the outer ones. Three agglomerations, Mikulčice-Valy, Staré Město–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uherske-hradiste">Uherské Hradiště</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nitra</a>, formed huge fortified pre-urban centres; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikulcice">Mikulčice</a>'s acropolis on an island in the Morava enclosed six hectares with a palace and twelve churches, and Nitra had some twenty craftsmen's villages. Around the forts lay villages growing wheat, barley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and farming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, pigs, sheep and horses no larger than Przewalski horses.</p><p>Two trade routes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the ancient Amber Road, crossed the region. Iron metallurgy and smithing were the chief industries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> had no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> or lead mines, so jewellery and weapons were made from metal won as loot, gift or trade; no local coinage is known, and axe-shaped ingots may have served as a premonetary currency. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a>, brocade and glass vessels were imported, and the Moravians paid chiefly in slaves taken as prisoners of war: Archbishop Thietmar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> accused them of “bringing noble men and honest women into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>” in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, and the Raffelstetten Customs Regulations record slaves sent west from Moravia. Most people were freemen paying an annual tax; slavery and feudal dependency are also recorded.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-the-german.jpg" alt="Louis the German" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludwig_der_Deutsche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The core of the army was the družina, a princely retinue of professional warriors, some as his permanent guard and the rest garrisoned in forts; Ruttkay estimates it at 3,000–5,000 men. Its permanent part was cavalry, heavy horsemen modelled on the Frankish forerunners of knights with equipment only the richest could afford. In large mobilisations the retinues of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> and the pohotovosť of free commoners joined, perhaps 20,000–30,000 men in all, fighting mainly in defence under the prince or a voivode. The foot soldier's weapon was the bradatica axe; double-edged swords spread, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> sabres and reflex bows are absent.</p><p>Fortifications were the backbone of defence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> invaded in 855 and withdrew before “strong fortifications”; in 864 he besieged Rastislav in Dowina and could not take it; in 869 Charles burnt the walled places of the region but failed before Rastislav's “huge fortification, quite unlike any built in olden times”. In 871 the Moravians rose against the Frankish governors William and Engelschalk, elected Slavomír duke and, under the returning Svatopluk, drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> out; in 872 Svatopluk's army defeated imperial troops and forced their withdrawal. He invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> during the Wilhelminer War of 882–884 with “troops from all the Slav lands”, seized a pagan prince on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, and in 892 withstood King Arnulf's invasion although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> horsemen rode with the Franks.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nitra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nitra.jpg" alt="Nitra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nitra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nitra_-_St._Emmeram%27s_Cathedral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ingo Mehling, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> was ruled by the House of Mojmír, a “wider kinship” rather than a line of fathers and sons: Svatopluk I was the only ruler succeeded by his son, Rastislav owed his throne to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, and Slavomír, an ordained priest, was elected duke when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> held Svatopluk in 871. Rulers were styled dux, occasionally rex or malik, and Pope Stephen V addressed Svatopluk as “King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>” in 885. Whether the realm was organised by kinship or by counties under zhupans is debated. The realm had two centres, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nitra</a> was held as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> by the heir, Svatopluk under his uncle Rastislav. A council of “leading men”, optimates, sat with the monarch.</p><p>Religion was bound up with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Frankish priests served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>'s interests, so in 861 Rastislav asked Pope Nicholas I for missionaries who knew Slavic and, receiving no answer, turned to Emperor Michael III, who sent Constantine and Methodius in 863. In 880 John VIII's bull Industriae tuae placed Svatopluk under papal protection and confirmed Methodius over all clergy, yet at Svatopluk's request named his opponent Wiching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Nitra. Methodius also gave Moravia its only surviving laws, the Nomocanon, a translation of Byzantine canon law, and the Court Law for the People. After his death in 885 Wiching persuaded the pope to restrict the Slavonic rite and Svatopluk to expel his disciples in 886.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius.jpg" alt="Cyril and Methodius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sv_Kiril_Metodij_Zahari_Zograf_Trojanski_mon_1848.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">painted by Zahari Zograf (Захарий Христович Димитров), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>'s lasting mark was made in letters. Constantine, later Saint Cyril, devised the Glagolitic alphabet, the first dedicated to a Slavic language, and with Methodius translated the Gospel and then, in Methodius's words, “all the Scriptures in full, save <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a>” into Old Church Slavonic, which became the fourth liturgical language of Christendom. Though the Slavic liturgy lasted only about 22 years in Moravia, the disciples expelled in 886 carried it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>; at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> Literary School Bulgarian disciples such as Naum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-ohrid">Clement of Ohrid</a> created the Cyrillic alphabet, which became the script of the Bulgarian Empire and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, and Old Church Slavonic remains the liturgical language of several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches.</p><p>Moravia's churches, more than 25 identified in the core lands, ranged from three-nave basilicas at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikulcice">Mikulčice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a> to rotundas; its granulated earrings and gilded buttons formed a recognisable jewellery style. Politically it was the first West Slavic state and reached early feudalism, the social basis of the later medieval kingdoms of the region. In the nineteenth century Great Moravia became a theme of Czech and Slovak romantic nationalism, in the twentieth it served a Czechoslovak identity, and it is named in the preamble of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>'s constitution; the double cross thought to have come with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> is in Slovakia's arms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-the-child.jpg" alt="Louis the Child" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichsschwert_ludwig_das_kind.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Svatopluk died in the summer of 894, and his son Mojmír II inherited a realm that soon came apart, as the tribes forced under Moravian rule shook it off. The Bohemian dukes accepted King Arnulf's suzerainty in June 895 and Mojmír's attempts to win them back failed. War with his younger brother Svatopluk II gave Arnulf a pretext to send troops in 898 and 899; Bavarian forces rescued the “boy” Svatopluk from the dungeon where his brother held him and, by Bartl's account, destroyed the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nitra</a>.</p><p>The Hungarians, beaten on the Pontic steppes around 895 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, were moving into the Carpathian Basin. Around 900 Moravians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> accused each other of swearing alliances with them “by the means of a dog and a wolf”, and a large Hungarian army “attacked and invaded” <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> in 900. Mojmír II made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a> in 901. The end cannot be dated: Róna-Tas puts the Hungarian occupation in 902, Spinei in 903 or 904, Spiesz the state's extinction in 907; the Raffelstetten Customs Regulations of 903–906 still speak of “the markets of the Moravians”, and no Moravian force fought at Brezalauspurc in 907, where the Hungarians routed the Bavarians. Its lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, the Slavic population stayed in the Carpathian valleys, Moravia is still mentioned in 924/925 and 942, and only the chapel at Kopčany still stands from its time.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, the first major West Slavic state, grew under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mojmir-i">Mojmír I</a>, Rastislav and Svatopluk I along the Morava River from the 830s, received the mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> in 863 and with it the Glagolitic alphabet and the Slavonic liturgy, reached its widest extent around 890, and fell to internal strife and the Hungarians between 902 and 907.</em></p>

